{:nav-title "Querying"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Querying Basics"]
  [:p "The query on the first page matched facts stored in our Pokemon database. "
   "Let's look at how those facts appear, and query them."]
  [:p "Each Pokemon is stored as an " [:strong "Entity"] ". Here is what Bulbasaur looks like:"]
  [:ui/code
   {:pokemon/name    "Bulbasaur",
    :pokemon/number  "001",
    :pokemon/type    ["Grass" "Poison"],
    :stat/attack     49,
    :stat/defense    49,
    :stat/hp         45,
    :stat/sp-attack  65,
    :stat/sp-defense 65,
    :stat/speed      45}]
  [:p "Each key is an " [:strong "Attribute"] " (:pokemon/name, :stat/speed, etc"
   ") and each value is the " [:strong "Value"] " for that attribute on this entity (Bulbasaur, 45, etc)."]
  [:p "Datomic assigns every entity a unique " [:strong "entity ID"] " when it is first added. "
   "You never choose it, and because of that, code shouldn't store an entity id outside Datomic. "
   "The " [:a {:href "/relationships"} "Relationships"] " chapter shows how to identify an entity a "
   "different way, with a unique attribute instead. "
   "(See " [:a {:href "https://docs.datomic.com/data-model.html"} "the Datomic data model"] ".) "
   "Let's find Bulbasaur's:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?entity-id
    :where [?entity-id :pokemon/name "Bulbasaur"]]]
  [:p "Now that we have the concept of an entity ID, we can flip the query around and ask: "
   "what Attributes does this entity have? "
   "We bind the attribute position to a logic variable, then resolve its human-readable name via "
   [:code ":db/ident"] ":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?attribute
    :where
    [?entity-id :pokemon/name "Bulbasaur"]
    [?entity-id ?attribute-id _]
    [?attribute-id :db/ident ?attribute]]]
  [:p "The underscore " [:code "_"] " in the third position is a wildcard, it matches any value but does not bind it to a variable."]
  [:p "Finally, we can pull all attribute-value pairs at once by binding the value position too:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?attribute ?value
    :where
    [?entity-id :pokemon/name "Bulbasaur"]
    [?entity-id ?attribute-id ?value]
    [?attribute-id :db/ident ?attribute]]]
  [:p "This works because of Datomic's "
   [:a {:href "https://docs.datomic.com/data-model.html#universal"} "Universal Schema"]
   ". Every fact in the database is stored following this Entity-Attribute-Value structure. "
   "Attributes are themselves entities, which is why we can traverse from "
   [:code "?attribute-id"] " to " [:code ":db/ident"] " to get the keyword name."]
  [:p "You may notice one more attribute for Bulbasaur, " [:code ":evolution/next"] ". "
   "Its value is a number: the entity ID of Ivysaur. This is a " [:strong "reference"]
   " from one entity to another. The " [:a {:href "/relationships"} "Relationships"] " chapter shows how to "
   "query references like this one, and the " [:a {:href "/pull"} "Pull"] " and " [:a {:href "/rules"} "Rules"]
   " chapters follow " [:code ":evolution/next"] " itself to walk Pokemon through their evolutions."]
  [:p "Try changing \"Bulbasaur\" to another Pokemon name in the queries above."]
  [:p "Take a look at the "
   [:a {:href "https://github.com/thiagooak/trydatomic/blob/main/resources/pokemon.edn?plain=1"} "EDN file"]
   " defining all of the Pokemon in our database. "
   "It is written as one transaction for each game generation, which the "
   [:a {:href "/time-travel"} "Time travel"] " chapter explains."]
  [:p "Run the last query with a dual-typed Pokemon like \"Charizard\" and observe "
   "how many attribute-value rows appear: one for each fact stored about that entity."]]}
